These findings are actionable runtime-safety regressions: one exposes filesystem traversal through the newly mounted SPA handler, and one adds mutable systemd control operations without enforcing authentication/authorization. Both directly weaken operational safety and should be remediated before considering the remediation pass complete.

Full review comments:

- [P1] Prevent path traversal in SPA catch-all file serving — /home/wali/Desktop/AI BOT REBUILD/v2/backend/app/main.py:210-212
  The SPA catch-all route builds `public_dir / full_path` directly from user input and only filters `api/` and `public/`; this still accepts `..` segments, so a request like `/{../../...}` can escape the intended mirror and expose files outside `v2/frontend/public` (e.g., repo secrets or credentials) through this unauthenticated HTTP surface.

- [P1] Protect service-control endpoint from unauthenticated systemctl access — /home/wali/Desktop/AI BOT REBUILD/v2/backend/app/api/v1/ingestors.py:222-231
  The new `/api/v1/ingestors/{service_id}/control` endpoint invokes `systemctl start|stop|restart` on arbitrary whitelisted service IDs, but the middleware stack is scaffolded no-op shells for auth/IP controls and this route has no additional permission check. This introduces an unauthorized runtime mutation surface (service restarts/starts) despite the existing safety constraints that only additive read-only controls were expected.
